<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman–Seleucid War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/192-bce">192</a>–<a href="/years/188-bce">188 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roman–Seleucid War was fought from 192 to 188 BCE between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, with its allies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, and the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III the Great</a>. Antiochus landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> but was defeated at Thermopylae in 191 BCE and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> at Magnesia in 190 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> (188 BCE) forced him to give up his lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> and pay a heavy indemnity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-seleucid-war.jpg" alt="Roman–Seleucid War" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en:William Robert Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asia_Minor_188_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>A &quot;cold war&quot; between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> had begun in 196 BCE. Rome saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> as its sphere of influence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> as a buffer, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> saw Asia Minor as a core part of his empire and Greece as the buffer. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus">Titus Quinctius Flamininus</a> proclaimed the freedom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, including those under Antiochus in Asia Minor, and in 195 BCE Roman forces largely withdrew from Greece.</p><p>Antiochus campaigned against tribes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and read the withdrawal as a retreat. In late 193 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>, hoping to draw both powers into a war for its own advantage, invited Antiochus to liberate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a> and said he would free the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from Roman subjugation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Antiochus was received coolly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and the Achaean League declared war on him. In the spring of 191 BCE the Macedonians entered the war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a>, and the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manius-acilius-glabrio">Manius Acilius Glabrio</a> marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. Antiochus chose to fight at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>, was overwhelmingly defeated and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, less than six months after his arrival at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a>. Roman fleets then defeated his navy off Corycus and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-myonessus">Battle of Myonessus</a>, and the Rhodians stopped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s fleet at the Eurymedon. The consul Lucius Cornelius Scipio and his legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> without opposition.</p><p>Antiochus offered to pay half the Roman costs and give up Smyrna, Lampsacus and Alexandria Troas, but the Scipios demanded all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> and the whole cost of the war. Around the middle of December 190 BCE the armies met near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Magnesia ad Sipylum</a>. Livy gives Antiochus 60,000 infantry and 12,000 cavalry against about 30,000, though John Grainger argues both had about 50,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of Pergamum led a cavalry charge, and when the Seleucid infantry was thrown into disorder by its own elephants Eumenes annihilated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> from the flank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>At Sardis Antiochus accepted terms later settled at Apamea in 188 BCE. He ceded his lands beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>, paid 15,000 Euboeic talents over twelve years, gave twenty hostages, including his youngest son, and was to surrender enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. His navy was limited to ten large ships and he was to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. The senate gave Rhodes Lycia and Caria south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a> and Eumenes the rest, and wished to keep no army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a>, who made a separate peace in 189 BCE, were reduced to a Roman client state. Hannibal fled and killed himself in 183 or 182 BCE, and Antiochus died on 3 July 187 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman–Seleucid war” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
